== To the Reader

Christian reader,

#smallcaps[If] you are in any measure awake in these days in which we live, and have taken notice of the many, great, and various temptations that beset all sorts of persons who know the Lord and profess his name, and to which they are continually exposed. You have seen too what success those temptations have had, to the unspeakable scandal of the gospel, and to the wounding and ruin of countless souls. If so, I suppose you will not look any further for other reasons for publishing the warnings and directions that follow, since they are suited to the times that pass over us and to your own concern in them. To those who think fit to keep asking, I will say only this. My first commitment to setting these meditations before the public did arise from the desires of some people. By their personal holiness, and by their constant adherence to everything that is made precious by its relation to Christ, they have openly stood for his interest in the world, and that has given them power over me to require services of greater importance at any time. Yet I dare not lay my doing it on that account in such a way as to suggest in the least that I did not myself consider it timely and necessary, in view of the general state of things I have mentioned. I have myself been exercised in this world with a variety of outward providences and dealings of God, and with the inward trials that came with them. Added to this is what I have had the opportunity to observe of the ways and walk of others: their beginnings, progress, and endings, their risings and falls, in profession and conduct, in darkness and light. All this has left on my mind and spirit such a constant sense and impression of the power and danger of temptations that, without any other pleas or pretexts, I must own it to have been needful in this season, in my own judgment, to give men a serious call to beware, and to uncover some of the most notable ways and means by which present temptations prevail.

But now, reader, perhaps you are among those who take no notice of these things, or do not care about them. You have no sense of the efficacy and dangers of temptations in your own walk and profession, and you have not observed their power over others. You do not discern the many advantages they have gained in these days, when all things are shaken, and you have not been troubled or moved by the sad successes they have had among professors. You suppose that all things are well, at home and abroad, and that they would be better still if you could get fuller satisfaction for some of your lusts in the pleasures or profits of the world. If so, I want you to know that I am not writing for you, and I do not consider you a fit reader or judge of what is written here. Consider the times. All the outcomes of God’s providential dealings with regard to the public affairs of these nations are perplexed and entangled; the footsteps of God lie in the deep, where his paths are not known. In particular, unparalleled distresses and strange prosperities are measured out to men, yes, to professors. A spirit of error, giddiness, and delusion goes out with such strength and efficacy that it seems to have received a commission to go and prosper. Such divisions, strifes, and rivalries are found among brethren, and with them such evil suspicions, wrath, and revenge. The desperate outcomes and products of men’s temptations are seen daily in partial and total apostasy, in the decay of love and the overthrow of faith; our days are filled with fearful examples of backsliding such as former ages never knew. There is a visible decline from reformation taking hold of the professing party of these nations, both in personal holiness and in zeal for the interest of Christ. While all this is so, the man who does not understand that an “hour of temptation” has come upon the world, “to test those who dwell on the earth,” is without doubt one of two things. Either he is himself at present held captive under the power of some woeful lust, corruption, or temptation, or he is indeed stark blind and does not know at all what it is to serve God in temptations. With such men, then, I have nothing to do at present. The warning that follows is intended for those who have a general sense of these things, and who are also able in some measure to consider that the plague has begun. It is meant to awaken them further to look about them. Perhaps the infection has come nearer to them, by some secret and imperceptible ways, than they realized; or perhaps they may be caught unawares later by some of those temptations that in these days either lay waste at noon or else walk in darkness. And the directions that follow are put forward for consideration for the sake of those who mourn in secret for all the abominations found among and upon those who profess the gospel. They are under the leadership of the Captain of their salvation, fighting and resisting the power of temptations, from whatever spring in themselves those temptations rise.

That our faithful and merciful High Priest, who both suffered and was tempted, and on that account is touched with the feeling of our weaknesses, would accompany this small discourse with timely supplies of his Spirit and fitting mercy to those who consider it, so that it may be useful to his servants for the ends for which it is designed, is the prayer of one who received this handful of seed from his storehouse and treasure.

#v(1em)

#align(right)[John Owen.]
